{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794751242,"nonce":"0v8NxyjwEKvmqdGjB71hpsmpkAo=","message":"Desktop baseline for the acceptance test (macOS, release, 720x480 window, 10 s after a 6 s warm-up, mean CPU in cores, two runs each; tools/blinc-bench css_window_check, ANIMATE and STATIC added for this).\n\n- empty window: 0.000. 100 static labelled cards, idle: 0.000.\n- one fading block: 0.096, 0.090.\n- three fading blocks over 100 static cards: 0.099, 0.089.\n- three spinning (translate, scale, rotate) cards over 100 static cards: 0.104, 0.103.\n- flow_demo (canvas flow shaders): 0.125, 0.179.\n\nThe animated cases do not depend on the size of the static interface. The motion trace of the three-fades scene (BLINC_MOTION_TRACE, 333 frames after 3 s, fps cap 60) has every frame on the fast path. A frame costs a median 0.46 ms of CPU in the loop (p95 0.60): 0.34 ms is the render (encode and submit), 0.026 ms is the CSS tick, 0.016 ms the style and layout gate, 0.004 ms the prelude. The work a clock frame would skip (tick, rebuild, walk) is about 0.05 ms of that. 0.46 ms at 48 frames a second is 0.02 cores; the other 0.08 is presentation and the driver, which a frame that is drawn at all pays.\n\nSo on desktop the per-frame CPU of a time-only effect is already close to the floor for a loop that presents every frame, and a clock-frame path would not move it measurably. What would: drawing fewer frames (the 20 fps cap for settled spring tails, aef5459, is that) or a cheaper submit. The place the number is still large is the Pixel: 1.3 to 1.5 cores at 120 fps for cards that cost 0.5 on the iPhone and 0.1 here. Not measured on a phone for a flow shader or a spinner.\n\nSuggest keeping P3 for wake scheduling (descriptor end, caret) and animation-play-state and prefers-reduced-motion, and dropping the clock-frame skip until a phone profile shows the loop, not the submit, is where the time goes.","files":null}]}